"A service mesh diagram separates workload traffic handling from the control plane that supplies identity, policy, and routing configuration.\n\nUse when: Explain mesh request paths and configuration distribution. Review proxy placement and control-plane dependencies.\n\nAvoid when: A simple service map is sufficient without mesh behavior.\n\nVisual grammar: Workload and proxy or node-proxy roles use distinct nodes. Data-plane links show application traffic paths. Control-plane links separately show policy, identity, and configuration distribution.\n\nBuild: Name the mesh implementation and deployment mode. Place workloads and the actual traffic interception components. Draw data paths before adding control distribution. Use separate corridors for control and data links and pad workload groups generously.\n\nCheck: Sidecar, ambient, or other modes follow the actual implementation. A control-plane link is not mistaken for every request’s data path. Identity and failure behavior are described from evidence. Proxy labels and control arrows do not intersect workload text.\n\nPitfalls: Assuming every mesh uses a sidecar per Pod. Treating encrypted mesh traffic as proof of application-level authorization.\n\nExample: Application traffic passes through the selected mesh proxies while a control plane distributes routing and identity configuration.\n\nAlternatives: Service dependency map, Zero-trust policy diagram."
